Improper Exception Handling Leading to Sensitive Information Disclosure

Severity

Low

CWE

CWE-209: Generation of Error Message Containing Sensitive Information

OWASP

OWASP ASVS V9.3 – Error Handling and Logging

Description

During security testing, the application generated an unhandled exception that displayed verbose debugging information directly to the user. Instead of presenting a generic error message, the application disclosed internal implementation details that should not be accessible to end users.

The exception window revealed sensitive application information including:

Internal namespace names
Class names
Method names
Source code paths
Line numbers
Stack trace
.NET framework information
Internal project directory structure
Module names
ViewModel names
Function names
Parameter names
Exception type (System.ArgumentNullException)
Application architecture details

Example information disclosed:

MorganStanley.Ird.HedgeSuMo.HeatMapModule.ViewModel.SubmissionViewModel
GetCalcServiceModifiedName()
SubmissionViewModel.cs
Line 939
System.Linq.Enumerable.FirstOrDefault()
Parameter name: source

The application also displayed options such as Continue, Terminate, Send Report, and Copy, allowing users to copy the entire stack trace.

Impact

An attacker can leverage the disclosed information to:

Understand the application's internal architecture.
Identify namespaces, classes, and methods.
Map the source code structure.
Identify business logic implementation.
Discover framework and technology stack details.
Assist in targeted exploitation of other vulnerabilities.
Facilitate reverse engineering of the application.

Although this issue does not directly allow unauthorized access, it significantly reduces the effort required for an attacker to perform further attacks.

Steps to Reproduce
Launch the Sumo thick client.
Perform the action that triggers the application exception (e.g., click the Send Email button under the tested conditions).
Observe that an unhandled exception dialog is displayed.
Expand the exception details.
Verify that the application exposes:
Stack trace
Source code path
Namespace names
Class names
Method names
Line numbers
Internal implementation details
